      "id": 1,
      "text": "Villa El Salvador began in 1971 as a squatted pueblo joven, created by migrant families fleeing Peru's central highlands.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_El_Salvador",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Residents of Villa El Salvador pioneered their own utilities, bringing electricity, water, and sewage to the community before municipal services arrived.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_El_Salvador",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1987 the community received the Prince of Asturias Award, honoring its self‑organized social programs and civic spirit.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_El_Salvador",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Villa El Salvador is twinned with Rezé in France and, since 2006, with Tübingen in Germany, fostering international cultural exchanges.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_El_Salvador",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The district is the southern terminus of Lima Metro Line One, marking the end of the city’s rapid transit system.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_El_Salvador",
